  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM MDT Sat May 30 2026\n\nLow pressure system will move northward through Wyoming today. \nWe should see rainfall on the back side of the low with \nappreciable rainfall amounts east of a line from Challis to Arco\nto Pocatello and Malad. Models continue to show a sharp drop \noff in rainfall amounts along and west of this line. Although \nlight rain will be possible through Monday over parts of the \narea, the more impressive amounts will fall today. 0.50 to 1.00 \ninches of rain is likely across the eastern highlands and the \nupper Snake Plain. On the western edge of the moisture, models \nare showing the potential for isolated thunderstorms this \nafternoon. Gusty winds are likely today across south Idaho, \nlikely resulting in a lake wind advisory for the American Falls \nReservoir today. Much cooler air will pour into the region today\nwith highs in the 50s across the eastern highlands and upper \nSnake Plain with 60s elsewhere. Temps will return to closer to \nnormal on Sunday with highs in the 50s for the eastern \nhighlands, 60s for the mountains and upper Snake River Plain, \nand lower 70s for the lower Snake River Plain and eastern Magic \nValley. OVernight lows going into Monday morning will flirt with\nfrost criteria, but will probably stay just warm enough. Expect\nupper 30s for lows across the Snake River Plain with a few \ncolder pockets possible. Temps return to the 80s by Tuesday. \nModels still show a weak trough passing through Wednesday or \nThursday which could produce some light rain for us. But there \nis some uncertainty in how deep that trough is. If the trough is\nshallower, the better rain amounts will stay north into \nMontana.",
